# Installed environments and downloaded references are large, machine-specific and
# non-relocatable. The RECIPES that build them are tracked - see conf/env/.
# `/env/` is root-anchored on purpose: an unanchored `env/` also matched conf/env/
# and silently excluded every install recipe, which is the one thing a reproducibility
# claim cannot afford to lose.
/env/
references/*/
!references/_registry/

# Per-cohort expectations describe data the reader does not have.
*.local.tsv
*.local.yml

# Run outputs.
work/
results/

# Python. `*.pyc.tmp` is the half-written byte-code CPython leaves when a compile is
# interrupted; four of them were committed and shipped to every user of this repository.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyc.tmp
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/

# Tool caches.
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Packaging output. Anchored to the root for the same reason `/env/` is: an unanchored
# `build/` would also match any directory named `build` inside the tree, and this
# repository already keeps a build script and a build recipe under tracked paths.
/build/
/dist/

# Editor and OS noise.
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store

# The blind-test handover. It names the calibration cohort, its libraries, its host and
# its numbers, none of which belong in a public general-purpose pipeline. It lives with the
# private harness that produced it.
HANDOVER.md

# Written by the deploy procedure so a compute node (which has no git) can record which commit
# ran. Generated, never edited, and untracked by design - but un-ignored it is permanent porcelain,
# which makes any "clean worktree" check unsatisfiable and trains its reader to skip the check.
HEAD.txt
